<header><p class="st-kicker">tribal confederation · Italy</p><h1 class="st-title">Messapians</h1><p class="st-meta">800 BCE – 266 BCE (approx.) · capital Uria</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Iapygian people of the Salento peninsula in Apulia, long-time rivals of Greek Taras, with their own language, fortified towns and kings.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The Messapians emerged in the heel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in the early 1st millennium BCE as one of three Iapygian peoples, with the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/daunians">Daunians</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/peucetians">Peucetians</a>. Ancient writers traced them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illyria">Illyria</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crete">Crete</a>; modern scholars note links across the Adriatic but see a largely local formation. Their main centres included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oria-apulia">Uria</a> (Oria), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudiae">Rudiae</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manduria">Manduria</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cavallino">Cavallino</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egnatia">Egnatia</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Messapian towns had massive stone walls, chamber tombs and painted trozzella vases. They wrote <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messapic-language">Messapic</a>, an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indo-european-languages">Indo-European</a> language, in an alphabet borrowed from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/taras">Taras</a>, and their many inscriptions are a key source. Kings are mentioned occasionally, like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artas">Artas</a>, who supplied javelin-men to <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> in 413 BCE. They inflicted a heavy defeat on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tarentum">Taras</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/rhegion">Rhegion</a> around 473 BCE, and at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manduria">Manduria</a> in 338 BCE they killed the Spartan king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/archidamus-iii">Archidamus III</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> defeated the Messapians and their Sallentini neighbours in 267–266 BCE, and their towns became allies and later municipia. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messapic-language">Messapic</a> was still written into the 1st century BCE before giving way to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a>. The poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ennius">Ennius</a>, born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rudiae">Rudiae</a>, was of Messapian background.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/messapians.jpg" alt="Messapians" width="320" height="240"><figcaption>Image: Original uploader was Galiano.M at it.wikipedia · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Messapian_pottery.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>800 BCE – 266 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>tribal confederation</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>tribal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Uria</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Artas</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Messapian polytheism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Messapic</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>Italy</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Messapii, Sallentini</dd></dl></aside></div>

